{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Lake Ontario shore trolley through the Town of Greece -- the lakeshore counterpart to the steam Hojack Line (hojack_line.geojson), which crossed Greece a couple of miles inland with stops at Greece and North Greece. This electric line ran 8 miles west from the village of Ontario Beach at the mouth of the Genesee, entirely within Greece apart from its Charlotte terminus, serving the beach hotels, cottages and picnic grounds along the shore. It opened in June 1891 as the Grand View Beach Railway and passed through two more owners -- the Rochester, Charlotte & Manitou Beach Railroad (1895-1908) and the Rochester & Manitou Railroad (1908-1925) -- before the last car ran in 1925. It is carried here as three features sharing one geometry, split at each change of owner per CLAUDE.md Rule 4. Charlotte was annexed by Rochester in 1916, so for most of the line's life a rider bound for the city changed at Ontario Beach rather than riding through.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_View_Beach_Railway (scenic electric street railway from the village of Ontario Beach, a suburb of Rochester, to Manitou Beach; 8 miles (13 km) long; operations commenced June 1891; last car ran 1925; subsequently operated by the Rochester, Charlotte & Manitou Beach Railroad 1895-1908 and the Rochester & Manitou Railroad 1908-1925; Rae motors and modern electrical equipment)", "https://www.frrandp.com/2019/07/a-railway-on-lake-ontario-grand-view.html (route detail: for several miles out of Ontario Beach the road runs along a bluff close to and overlooking the lake, about 15 feet above water level; further west the track threads a sandy beach between the Great Lake and various little bays and ponds; trestles over water in spots including one over Braddock's Bay)"], "geometry_sources": ["OpenStreetMap via Overpass (maps.mail.ru mirror, 2026-08-24): highway geometry for Beach Avenue, Edgemere Drive and Manitou Beach Road, chained by shortest path. A query for railway=abandoned/razed/dismantled across the whole corridor returned only the New York Central's Hojack ways -- nothing of this line's own right of way survives in OSM tagging. See each feature's geometry_quality."], "not_yet_sourced": "Service frequency in any era -- the 60-minute headway is a guess, and a summer beach trolley very likely ran much more often in season than in winter. Intermediate stop names beyond Crescent Beach and Manitou Beach. Whether through cars ever ran into Rochester proper over the city street railway, or whether every rider changed at Ontario Beach.", "pulled": "20260824", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6.", "interurban_audit": "2026-08-24: features on this layer that are inter-city electric railways were retagged mode='interurban' (previously 'streetcar' or 'intercity_rail') so the map can draw them distinctly from city streetcars and from steam roads. See CLAUDE.md Rule 7."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Grand View Beach Railway", "line": "Grand View Beach: Ontario Beach (Charlotte) \u2013 Manitou Beach", "id": "gvb-ontario-beach-manitou", "color": "#00A6A6", "mode": "interurban", "avg_speed_kmh": 24, "speed_source": "estimated: open-car electric street railway on a shore alignment; unsourced", "geometry_quality": "Approximate. The right of way was lifted in 1925-26 and is not tagged as a railway anywhere in OpenStreetMap, so this is traced along the shore-road corridor the line ran on or beside: 2.06 km on Beach Avenue out of Ontario Beach, then 7.49 km on Edgemere Drive along the bluff and the barrier beach between Lake Ontario and Long Pond, Cranberry Pond and Braddock Bay, then a 1.71 km straight leg standing in for the trestle across the mouth of Braddock Bay -- no modern road crosses there -- and 0.19 km on Manitou Beach Road to the Manitou Beach terminus. Two further chords of 0.49 km and 0.30 km bridge gaps in the Edgemere Drive geometry. Traced length 11.44 km (7.11 miles) against a documented 8 miles: the shortfall is mostly the straight bay crossing and the barrier-beach wiggle the roads do not reproduce.", "segments": [{"start": 1891, "end": 1895, "headway_min": 60, "note": "Opened June 1891 as a scenic electric street railway along the Lake Ontario shore, 8 miles from the village of Ontario Beach to Manitou Beach. For the first few miles out of Ontario Beach it ran on a bluff about 15 feet above the water; further west it threaded the sandy barrier beach between the lake and the ponds and bays, crossing Braddock's Bay on a trestle. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_View_Beach_Railway", "https://www.frrandp.com/2019/07/a-railway-on-lake-ontario-grand-view.html"]}]}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-77.60861, 43.25828], [-77.60857, 43.25834], [-77.60856, 43.25839], [-77.60858, 43.25845], [-77.60862, 43.25849], [-77.60867, 43.25852], [-77.60874, 43.25857], [-77.60912, 43.2588], [-77.60931, 43.2589], [-77.60952, 43.25903], [-77.60965, 43.25911], [-77.60979, 43.25921], [-77.60985, 43.25925], [-77.61058, 43.25973], [-77.61069, 43.2598], [-77.61142, 43.26025], [-77.61183, 43.2605], [-77.61336, 43.26152], [-77.61455, 43.26232], [-77.61504, 43.26264], [-77.61546, 43.26293], [-77.6156